::CW_U06_L04_Q01::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A poem says, "I was sad." Feedback asks for stronger imagery. Which revision best fits?{
~A. I was sad and very sad.#Incorrect. This repeats the emotion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Sad means unhappy.#Incorrect. This defines rather than revises. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Rain gathered on the sill until the glass forgot the street.#Correct. This uses image to suggest emotion. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Delete the poem.#Incorrect. The line can be revised. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q02::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which line break revision best slows hesitation?{
=A. Put "stayed still" on its own line after "my hand."#Correct. This creates a pause before the final action.
~B. Put breaks after every letter.#Incorrect. Random breaks distract. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Remove all line breaks.#Incorrect. Line breaks can control pacing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add breaks with no reason.#Incorrect. Breaks should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q03::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which style revision is most precise?{
~A. Replace every word with a longer word.#Incorrect. Longer does not mean better. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Replace "walked slowly" with "shuffled" when the speaker is tired.#Correct. The verb is precise and supports meaning.
~C. Add vague words like good and nice.#Incorrect. Vague words weaken style. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Ignore tone.#Incorrect. Style should support tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q04::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A poem repeats "cold" seven times; feedback says the repetition becomes distracting. What should the writer do?{
~A. Add ten more repetitions.#Incorrect. That worsens the issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove the poem.#Incorrect. The repetition can be revised. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Change the title only.#Incorrect. The issue is repetition. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Keep the strongest repetitions and replace or remove distracting ones.#Correct. This preserves purpose while improving control. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q05::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which explanation best connects line break to effect?{
=A. I placed the final phrase on its own line so the reader pauses before the decision.#Correct. This explains craft and reader effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. I used breaks because poems need random spaces.#Incorrect. Breaks should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I changed it because I had to.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I made the poem look unusual.#Incorrect. Appearance alone is not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q06::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which problem is about figurative language?{
~A. A word is spelled correctly.#Incorrect. This is not a problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The title is clear.#Incorrect. That is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. A simile compares two things in a way that confuses the poem's meaning.#Correct. This is a figurative-language problem. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The line breaks support pacing.#Incorrect. That is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q07::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best improves sound?{
~A. Add random rhymes that change the meaning.#Incorrect. Sound should not damage meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Add purposeful repetition of a soft sound to support a quiet tone.#Correct. Sound devices should match effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Remove all sound awareness.#Incorrect. Sound matters in poetry. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add unrelated capitals.#Incorrect. Capitals do not create controlled sound. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q08::[MLA.CW.REV.01] What should happen before revising line breaks?{
~A. Add random spaces first.#Incorrect. Random formatting is weak. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Ignore meaning.#Incorrect. Meaning guides revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Delete all images.#Incorrect. Images may be central. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Identify the intended effect or emphasis.#Correct. Breaks should support purpose. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q09::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which portfolio explanation is strongest?{
=A. I revised the image so readers feel grief through the window scene rather than direct statement.#Correct. This names revision and effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. It is good.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I changed some words.#Incorrect. This lacks detail. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I like it.#Incorrect. Preference is not craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q10::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which answer is a weak style revision?{
~A. Replace a vague emotion with a concrete image.#Incorrect. This is strong. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Replace a precise verb with a vague word like "went."#Correct. This weakens style by reducing precision. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Revise line breaks for pacing.#Incorrect. This is strong if purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove distracting repetition.#Incorrect. This is targeted revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q11::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which craft feature is most connected to where a poetic line ends?{
~A. Character motivation.#Incorrect. That is narrative craft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Plot twist.#Incorrect. That is story structure. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Line break.#Correct. A line break is the ending of a poetic line.
~D. Dialogue tag.#Incorrect. That is prose dialogue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q12::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A style piece sounds flat because every sentence has the same length. Which revision fits?{
=A. Vary sentence length to control rhythm.#Correct. Sentence rhythm affects style. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Make every sentence identical.#Incorrect. That keeps the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Remove all punctuation.#Incorrect. This may confuse readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add random rhymes.#Incorrect. That does not solve sentence rhythm. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q13::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best supports quiet grief?{
~A. Add five exclamation points.#Incorrect. This may break the quiet tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Turn the poem into a joke.#Incorrect. That changes the purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Remove all emotion.#Incorrect. The goal is controlled emotion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Use a restrained image, such as an untouched coat by the door.#Correct. A quiet image supports the intended tone.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q14::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which statement best explains style growth?{
~A. I made it better.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I used a sharper verb so the speaker's hesitation becomes visible.#Correct. This names a style choice and effect.
~C. I changed words randomly.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I used long words to sound smart.#Incorrect. Style is precision, not display. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q15::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which is a poetry revision mistake?{
~A. Using a concrete image.#Incorrect. That can strengthen poetry. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Explaining reader effect.#Incorrect. That is useful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Adding random line breaks because the page should look unusual.#Correct. Random breaks weaken control. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Revising repetition when it distracts.#Incorrect. That is targeted. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q16::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best improves an unclear metaphor?{
=A. Clarify the comparison so readers understand what qualities connect.#Correct. This improves figurative meaning. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Add more confusing comparisons.#Incorrect. That increases confusion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Ignore reader confusion.#Incorrect. Revision should improve access. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove all meaning.#Incorrect. Poetry still needs meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q17::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which answer best identifies reader effect?{
~A. The poem is on a page.#Incorrect. This is not effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The writer used a keyboard.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The piece has a file name.#Incorrect. This is not reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
